  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">open2c/distiller-nf: v0.3.4 (2022) in Zenodo. 390 downloads.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">open2c/distiller-nf: v0.3.4 (2022) in Zenodo is authored by Goloborodko, Anton, Venev, Sergey and Spracklin, George. Topics: Nucleofection, Gestational period, TSG101, Diafiltration and Dysgeusia.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">4 citations (OpenAlex). Citations peaked in 2024 with 2 citing works. Citing venues include bioRxiv (Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory) and Nature Cell Biology.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Limited online visibility beyond citations.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "SoftwareStory",
  "publisher": "CERN",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">open2c/distiller-nf: v0.3.4 shows early but concrete uptake: it has 4 OpenAlex citations, 6 documented downstream reuses, and measurable use on Zenodo with 390 downloads and 2,842 views. The record points to a software release that is being adopted within genomics and chromatin research, especially through preprints and a peer-reviewed article.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This Zenodo release of distiller-nf is an open software version update that addresses dependency and workflow issues while adding support for deduplication and fastp. The stewardship record links it to a GitHub source release and to a broader versioned software lineage, indicating that it functions as part of an actively maintained toolchain rather than a stand-alone archive copy.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal is use in downstream research. OpenAlex shows 4 citations, and the citing landscape is concentrated in genomics and chromatin biology, with most citing works appearing as bioRxiv preprints and one appearing in Nature Cell Biology. That pattern is consistent with a tool being taken up in an active methodological area before citations accumulate broadly.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Reuse evidence strengthens that picture. The record identifies 6 downstream reuse instances, including related Zenodo software versions and biomedical mentions, suggesting that the release is part of an iterative software ecosystem. scite also records 2 mentions, adding a modest independent signal that the software is being noticed in the literature.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage metrics show that the release has been accessed beyond citation-bearing contexts: Zenodo reports 390 downloads and 2,842 views. Combined with the downstream citations, this suggests the software is being discovered and used, even though the scholarly citation base remains relatively small. Public discourse data were not available in the archive, so the record should be read as an early-stage impact profile grounded in usage and reuse rather than broad public attention.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
